{"conversation":"Maybe instead of checking every substring by reversing it, I could try to expand from the middle of the string towards the outside to find palindromes. So for each character (or pair of characters) in the string, I try to expand as far as the substring is palindrome. That way, I don't have to check every substring explicitly, only the palindromes centered at each point.","python_code":"class Solution:
    def longestPalindrome(self, s: str) -> str:
        longest = ""
        for i in range(len(s)):
            # Odd length palindromes
            l, r = i, i
            while l >= 0 and r < len(s) and s[l] == s[r]:
                if (r - l + 1) > len(longest):
                    longest = s[l:r+1]
                l -= 1
                r += 1
            # Even length palindromes
            l, r = i, i+1
            while l >= 0 and r < len(s) and s[l] == s[r]:
                if (r - l + 1) > len(longest):
                    longest = s[l:r+1]
                l -= 1
                r += 1
        return longest"}

